 ### Face-Block Finite State Machine
-A finite state machine that tracks isosurface topology evolution across a face block containing a face saddle, using sequential vertex sweeps to generate and disambiguate topological cases [S2]. It subdivides the face block into four simple blocks via planes through the face saddle perpendicular to the face. Vertices on the $x=0$ plane get the isovalue $f$ (face saddle), while points on the upper saddle plane get values between $u$ and $l$ [S2]. The sweep starts at case 0. Sweeping past vertex $0_{ul}$ or $0_{lu}$ generates case 1 in one sub-block and case 0 in three others, yielding overall case 1. Sweeping past the other of $0_{lu}/0_{ul}$ generates Nielson's case 3.0 (two separate surfaces). Sweeping past $f$ generates case 5 in two sub-blocks and case 8 in the other two, yielding overall case 3.1 (single tent-like surface). Sweeping past $u$ generates cases $1^*C$, $2^*C$, $2^*C$, and 5, yielding overall case $1^*C$. The process continues until case $0^*C$ is reached [S2]. This method uses the face saddle to disambiguate between topological cases 3.0 and 3.1, functioning identically to the Asymptotic Decider [S2].
+A finite state machine that tracks isosurface topology evolution across a face block containing a face saddle, using sequential vertex sweeps to generate and disambiguate topological cases [S2]. It subdivides the face block into four simple blocks via planes through the face saddle perpendicular to the face [S2]. Vertices on the $x=0$ plane get the isovalue $f$ (face saddle), while points on the upper saddle plane get values between $u$ and $l$ [S2]. The sweep starts at case 0. Sweeping past vertex $0_{ul}$ or $0_{lu}$ generates case 1 in one sub-block and case 0 in three others, yielding overall case 1 [S2]. Sweeping past the other of $0_{lu}/0_{ul}$ generates Nielson's case 3.0 (two separate surfaces) [S2]. Sweeping past $f$ generates case 5 in two sub-blocks and case 8 in the other two, yielding overall case 3.1 (single tent-like surface) [S2]. Sweeping past $u$ generates cases $1^*C$, $2^*C$, $2^*C$, and 5, yielding overall case $1^*C$ [S2]. The process continues until case $0^*C$ is reached [S2]. This method uses the face saddle to disambiguate between topological cases 3.0 and 3.1, functioning identically to the Asymptotic Decider [S2].
 
