+A prolongation operator for voxel simplification that replaces the identity corner weight with Catmull-Clark subdivision weights to approximate B-spline functions of higher order, aiming to smooth details more naturally than trilinear interpolation [S5, unofficial]. For a voxel corner, set $n=8$. Identify the 3 edge points $E$ (corners connected by edges) and 3 face points $F$ (corners sharing a face but not an edge) [S5, unofficial]. Exclude the diagonal point inside the volume [S5, unofficial]. Calculate $q$ using the formula:
 $$ q = \frac{F}{n} + \frac{2E}{n} + \frac{P(n-3)}{n} $$
 where $q$ is the new calculated vertex, $n$ is the number of vertices (8 for a voxel corner), $F$ is the average of all adjacent face points, $E$ is the average of all adjacent edge points, and $P$ is the original point [S5, unofficial]. Tests on the dragon dataset showed similar RMSE results to adaptive simplification but worse memory compression rates [S5, unofficial]. This is because the initial function is trilinear, while Catmull-Clark approximates higher-order B-splines [S5, unofficial].
